                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "You are Angelica listening to your grandmother reminiscing about her school days.  She mentioned that the headmistress was a witch and as she speaks her name, the witch materialises in the room!  You come between grandma and the witch and lose your youth in the process.  To gain it back you have to go back to grandma's old school.  The story line expands as you meet other characters, but I won't spoil it for you.\nThis is a very short HO....only took 5 hours.  There is no bonus chapter despite it being a collector's edition...no extras at all!  This game is a classic example of having great potential, but falls flat.  Graphics are beautiful in a decaying way. \nMusic was a bit annoying at times, especially during HO scenes where it was almost like rock music.  Very jarring to say the least!\nThere is no map in this game.  It really needs it because there is a lot of needless back tracking....and this is what pads the game out.\nHO scenes are decent. There are no collectibles nor any morphing objects....so a very basic HO game.  Puzzles are adequate but fairly easy to do.\nThere are no voiceovers....just pop up dialogue.  I found starting and exiting the game to be very clunky.  Takes about 15 seconds to load up and shut down....and you're staring at a black screen with music in the background.\nSome HO objects were lost in translation e.g.  a safety pin was called a clothespin.  There is another blunder but I can't remember what object it was.\nSo overall, this was an average game which missed the potential to be great.  Very short, so buy on sale.  6.5/10",
